WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.055
YURI GRINSHTEYN: We use
SLOs in error budgets

00:00:02.055 --> 00:00:04.620
to set appropriate reliability
targets for our services.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:06.275
But what happens when
there's an issue?

00:00:06.275 --> 00:00:07.650
When you set up
a learning to let

00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:09.478
us know that users are unhappy?

00:00:09.478 --> 00:00:11.770
Today I want to show you how
to use error budget burner

00:00:11.770 --> 00:00:13.710
alerts in Service Monitoring.

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:15.090
This is the Stack Doctor.

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:18.401
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:22.037 --> 00:00:23.870
We've covered using
threshold-based alerting

00:00:23.870 --> 00:00:27.295
before, but learning on
SLOs is a bit different.

00:00:27.295 --> 00:00:28.670
The main thing to
keep in mind is

00:00:28.670 --> 00:00:31.490
that we want our alert to fire
long before our services out

00:00:31.490 --> 00:00:35.210
of SLO, essentially as soon as
we start burning error budget.

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:37.580
To start, let's cover some math.

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:40.400
To help us, let's review chapter
five of the SRE workbook,

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:42.010
called Alerting on SLOs.

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:43.760
If you haven't read
it before, no worries.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:46.130
We'll link to it in the
episode notes below.

00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:48.800
As covered in that chapter,
your learning strategy

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:50.540
should include four
considerations.

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:54.110
They are precision-- what
fraction of your alerts

00:00:54.110 --> 00:00:57.140
are actually triggered by a
real or significant event?

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:01.100
Recall-- what fraction of your
events results in an alert?

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:03.380
Detection time-- how long
does it take for an alert

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:05.510
to fire once an event starts?

00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:08.570
And reset time-- how long
after the event is over

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:11.520
does it take for
your alert to reset?

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:13.770
There's no right values
for these considerations.

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:16.140
It all depends on what
you want out of alerts.

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:18.930
However, you'll probably want to
find the right balance for you

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:20.880
while minimizing your
operational load--

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:22.630
that is, the number
of alerts you actually

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:24.600
receive and have to respond to.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:27.090
The best way to do this is
by alerting on error budget

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:30.282
burn rate, which is
where the math comes in.

00:01:30.282 --> 00:01:31.740
Error budget burn
rate is a measure

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:33.890
of how fast,
relative to your SLO,

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:35.850
your service consumes
error budget.

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:38.430
For example, a service that
has exactly zero budget

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:40.560
left at the end of the
SLO evaluation period

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:42.060
has a burn rate of one.

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:44.640
A service that consumes
all of its error budget

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:46.710
halfway through the
evaluation period

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:49.750
has a burn rate
of two, and so on.

00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:52.800
We calculate our burn rate by
looking at three main inputs--

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:54.930
the SLO and its
evaluation period,

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:57.390
the amount of error
budget consumed or burned,

00:01:57.390 --> 00:01:59.310
and the window over
which that burn happens.

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:01.545
That's the alerting window.

00:02:01.545 --> 00:02:02.920
This, then, is
the equation we'll

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:05.950
use to calculate burn rate that
we'll use when we configure

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:07.960
our error budget burn alerts.

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:10.509
Our burn rate is the amount
of error budget consumed

00:02:10.509 --> 00:02:13.960
by the service multiplied
by the SLO evaluation period

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:16.440
and divided by the
alerting window.

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:16.940
OK.

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:18.000
That was a lot.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.630
If you're a bit lost at this
point, that's totally OK.

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:22.010
All you really
need to understand

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:24.500
is that we can calculate
the error budget burn rate

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:27.410
if we know what our SLO is and
how much of our error budget

00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:31.890
we want to consume and over what
time before firing our alert.

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:33.865
The next question you
might have is, well,

00:02:33.865 --> 00:02:35.490
how much error budget
should my service

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:37.650
burn before firing an alert?

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:39.960
Here, we again return
to the alerting on SLOs

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:43.540
chapter of the SRE workbook
for two simple recommendations.

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:46.080
They are 2% of error
budget in one hour

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:48.570
and 5% of error
budget in six hours.

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:51.330
This way, you can both catch
higher error rates quickly

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:54.120
and lower error rates over
time without overwhelming

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:57.200
the on-callers with alerts.

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:59.152
Let's walk through
a specific example.

00:02:59.152 --> 00:03:00.860
Let's say our service
has an availability

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:04.202
SLO of 95% over a
rolling 28-day window,

00:03:04.202 --> 00:03:05.660
and we want to
create an alert that

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:08.270
lets us know if we've consumed
2% of our error budget

00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:10.390
in the last hour.

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:12.860
We use our simple equation
to calculate the burn rate

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:15.580
we need to hit, which is 13.44.

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:17.830
Let's use the same equation
to calculate the burn rate

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:21.760
threshold for our other alert,
5% of our budget in six hours.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:25.120
This time, our rate is 5.6.

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:25.620
OK.

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:27.540
So far, we've covered
our budget burn rates

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:28.920
and how we calculate them.

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:31.320
We're now ready to
set up our alerts.

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:33.852
Here's our service with our
SLO configured against it.

00:03:33.852 --> 00:03:35.310
We're going to
create an alert that

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:38.550
lets us know when the service
consumes 2% of its error budget

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:40.070
in an hour.

00:03:40.070 --> 00:03:42.920
This is where our budget
burn rate becomes important.

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:45.740
To configure the alert, we need
to specify the lookback window.

00:03:45.740 --> 00:03:47.550
In our case, it's 60 minutes.

00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:49.260
We also need to
specify the burn rate

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:52.200
we expect to hit during the
window as our threshold.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:55.460
Remember, we already
calculated that to be 13.44.

00:03:55.460 --> 00:03:57.200
Now, when our
service has an issue,

00:03:57.200 --> 00:03:58.940
we'll see the error
budget decrease,

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:01.340
and we'll get an alert that
looks like this, showing us

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:04.570
the error budget burn
during the incident.

00:04:04.570 --> 00:04:05.780
Thanks for joining me today.

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:06.653
We covered a lot.

00:04:06.653 --> 00:04:08.320
We learned about the
considerations that

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:10.240
should go into your
learning strategies,

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:11.980
and why alerting on
error budget burn

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:15.400
is a great way to balance them
against operational overload.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:17.560
We then figured out how
to calculate their budget

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:19.510
burn rates, and
saw how to use them

00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:22.540
to set up alerting for your
services in Service Monitoring.

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:24.978
Thanks so much for watching,
come back soon for more.

00:04:24.978 --> 00:04:26.020
This is the Stack Doctor.

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:27.010
Stay healthy out there.

00:04:27.010 --> 00:04:30.360
[MUSIC PLAYING]

